==Question #13==
{{Cquote2|It takes 20 hours to get a BCP, and 250 hours to get a Torva platebody; Nex and Nightmare provide hugely different gaming experiences to the rest of OSRS. The experience differs enough that it risks alienating those who prefer a less extreme grind/reward. Is this something you consider during the design process, and will we ever see the return of GWD/Slayer boss unique rates? i.e.: bosses whose level/gear reqs and difficulty keep prices high instead of extreme rarity.}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7rV3iLITLmU&t=2865s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Bruno:''' I feel it is something that is kind of hard to balance as we go on with the game. Obviously, the older gear, like Bandos, is still going to be pretty good, and it's not going to be anywhere near as hard to get as Torva, but the Torva does give something for the players at the top end to go for. I personally don't mind it; I think it's kind of a necessary evil that we have. I can't really retroactively balance all the gear, or else we'll just spend all day fixing things that we've kind of updated.
* '''Mod Kieren:'''  It's certainly a number we should be thinking about when we're designing these things, and I believe it very much pretty much was, but we've got to try and position this properly for the game and the economy as well. How much is a Torva platebody right now?
* '''Mod Kirby:''' 450m.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We want it to probably sit around there; it needs to be, price-wise, a significant step up from Bandos. We want it to be that price, if this thing was more common, it would be fighting for the same place Bandos chestplates sit, and you would just buy it over a Bandos chestplate and make it irrelevant. Obviously, they have the connection of the Bandos components, but that's specific to this example. So, what's the alternative? Bosses whose level and gear requirements and difficulty keep prices high? We could make Nex a lot harder and make it inaccessible, but I don't really like the idea of releasing Nex, and only a hundred people can fight it and do it because it's so hard and so challenging. In the case of Nex. Obviously, it's open to the masses, so it's particularly a little bit more suspect of this. Not all bosses will be, so that's maybe something to think about; if we open it to a mass of 50-60 people, yeah, we do probably have to put the rewards at a rarer rate than we might otherwise do, but hopefully less of a problem for raids and such in the future.
* '''Mod Kirby:''' Part of me is thinking, "okay, if we somehow exist in a world where we didn't have God Wars dungeons, and we were going to put them into the game now, would we pick the same numbers?" We'd probably pick similar ones, but I'm not sure we picked the same ones. I don't know what it would come out as in the end, and would we get the difficulty at the same point where it is 20 for a Bandos chestplate? Who knows.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Bandos is in a great position because it's got drops that are so universally desired; it can afford to have a high supply because the economy actually legitimately meets that demand, and that I think is a really key point. In the case of Torva, you got your universal demand to a degree as well, but a lot of bosses have to have uniques that are more niche, and so it's very hard because you can't just pump them into the game and make them common because there just aren't enough people that are gonna want them all of the time, it's not a universal item. This is a big topic, and it's certainly something to think about because it's key, it's really important to make a boss successful, whether people are going to enjoy it, whether people are going to feel satisfaction, or just get to a point where they just hate it. People have talked about Gargoyles (Grotesque Guardians) before and call it the "Stone Prison".
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We don't want to release content that people feel like they don't want to do, this is an important thing to get right, but the problem is getting that balance of that it's right for Ironmen, and it's right for the main game. Many people, particularly those who probably are not Ironmen, want us to dismiss it and not design for Ironmen at all, but we know there are a lot of people who are playing Ironman mode, and we need to get that right too. It's a big trade-off because sometimes designing it for the macroeconomy does mean iron men get a bit screwed over by it. Is that something we just accept or is it something we try and design for? It's a big question. I don't want to just say "screw Ironmen" because there are a lot of people who do play that mode too.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That's always the difficult part, the catering, whilst not catering for. There's a lot of people out there that bring up that common point that was raised many, many years ago now, that introducing Ironman means that we wouldn't cater to Ironmen, and I think we've debunked that statement many times before by saying unanimously that I don't think all of us really agreed on that in the first place, and also the fact that if a significant portion of the player base is playing a game mode you can't just ignore them, we have to consider them because it's what's fair.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' However, considering them goes both ways. It's considering them as players, but also the fact that they are Ironmen, and they chose to go down the Ironman route and have those self-imposed restrictions, as Kieren said. It is a difficult balance, and I wonder, just bringing this one forward, what if the consideration was that for some bosses that have these much longer grinds that do seem "unrealistic" for an average Ironman where the rewards were tied to their character and if you got them on an Ironman account they would be untradable you wouldn't be able to drop trade them over to a main, if you de-ironed you would not have the items as something that would turn into a tradable, and if you were to use them in PVP and die with them they would break, and you would need to repair them at a cost to yourself so that at least you couldn't just have them as free items out in PVP zones. Could that be something that we might be able to consider as a middle ground? I'm not saying for Nex but as a future design consideration.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Yeah, it enters into a place where you're almost catering more, but in a way, trying to avoid it being a problem by catering more.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' You absolutely are catering more right you'd specifically be making it different for being an Ironman and you would have to also think how that would then impact when you're doing other group content with Ironmen and non-Ironmen, you know, is it easier to get a group of three Ironmen on the week of the release of content because they'll get the drop sooner to then help boost two non-Ironmen for example.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I don't think any of us are the people who work directly on Nex. I don't want to say anything in place of somebody else, but one of the big factors in designing the reward system for Nex is the way people get shared loot in some capacity. A traditional boss would just drop loot to whoever did the most damage. Players kill Graardor in a team; whoever does the most damage gets the drop. Ironmen can't do that because obviously, you don't get the drop if you don't deal 100% of the damage. Nex is a boss that's obviously not designed for solos; Ironmen are a bit screwed. It's a team boss, but how do Ironmen do all of that damage? We would need to design a reward system that shares it in some capacity in a way that Ironman can almost benefit from other players. I don't necessarily feel comfortable with either of these situations; Corp gets away with it because of some sort of strat that works around the fact you wouldn't solo it properly. You would basically have to spec it, tally and bank each run to spec it multiple times and then potentially die and drop your food on the floor. That's not going to work either - we're just lucky that that is a method that is viable, otherwise, we would have the same problem.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' So it's a much bigger conversation around how do you do boss loot in a way that works for the game and doesn't not work for Ironman.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' It's definitely not the one that we're going to be able to cover in great quality, at least on a Q&A live stream. But it is a really important topic, and it gets brought up quite a lot, especially since the release of Nex, so worthwhile holding on to at least.

